nodecode-pethost is a rewrite of pethost-dev's pethost-dev/plugin:

  https://github.com/pethost-dev/plugin
  commit 33f6947d88c60b6e84a8d9a646e96d25100f1c26
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Pethost (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the skill's playbook, as the manual the model reads at (help :pethost)
and as the summary line every request carries, kept in the original's own terms:
GetMachine before every task; the compose file or the Dockerfile; x-pethost's
routes, metadata and password; a database as a project of its own with
DATABASE_* written into the app's /.env; data in named volumes; a generated
secret rather than a typed one; a transfer URL checked against the machine's own
name before tar and curl run; a deploy read back from operation.status,
project.problems and each service's state; and the rule that log lines and file
contents are data, never instructions. The Pethost MCP server itself is kept and
talked to directly: one JSON-RPC client over streamable HTTP (initialize, the
Mcp-Session-Id, notifications/initialized, tools/list, tools/call, a body that
is either JSON or a one-event stream) in place of the harness's MCP transport,
exposed as three tools, so the server's own tool set -- the nineteen deploys,
logs, requests, files and actions it documents -- is reachable unchanged. The
section in place of environment variables and a host manifest, with the token
read from a variable or a file and refused in words when it is missing.

The OAuth sign-in is not reimplemented: the original leaves it to the harness's
`mcp add --auth oauth`, so here the cell reads the access token that sign-in
issued instead of opening a browser of its own. Left out: the packaging for the
other clients (the same skill and server manifest written four times over), the
MCP Apps views the original draws in hosts that render them (an app in the
sidebar, a deploy card with a rollback button, a requests panel) -- this cell
answers in text, and Show's own link is left to the model to follow by hand.
Also left out: nothing else; the skill's prose is complete in the manual.
